From: John Merchant <john@johnmerchant.dev>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 03:24:07 +1030
Subject: [PATCH 31/42] hw/arm/chumby_classic: Add the sensor card on CSPI1

The stock kernel's chumby_accel module drives the sensor card on CSPI1
(SPI_CHAN 0). The card's two select lines are GPIOs, PD28 (SS0) and
PD27 (SS1), which the driver sets by hand and the card decodes into
four chip selects: SS1:SS0 = 2 is the Kionix accelerometer, 1 the
Atmel AT25080A "DC ID" EEPROM, and 3 is idle, which the driver always
passes through between the two.

Wire that up: the GPIO block's PD28 and PD27 outputs feed a decoder on
the board that drives the accelerometer's and the EEPROM's chip
selects, and CSPI1 hands them bytes (its "bytewise" mode), since the
driver uses 8, 16 and 32-bit words for byte-wide protocols.

The accelerometer is /machine/accel. The machine properties accel-x,
accel-y and accel-z, in milli-g, set its x-mg, y-mg and z-mg, at
startup (-M) or at run time (qom-set /machine accel-x 500). They start
at 0, 0, 1000: 1 g on Z, as for the part lying flat. Which way up the
card sits in a Chumby isn't known; that is a guess, and the stock
software doesn't settle it.

The EEPROM gets contents in the stock layouts, consistent with the
device identity:

- 0x300: the revision, 00.00.MAJOR.MINOR from the hardware version, and
  0x304: the last 12 characters of the serial number, which the driver
  reads into /proc/DCversion
- 0x000: what the stock "dcid" tool reads (dcid -o, sent with the
  control panel request by download_cp): the magic "sexi", one tag and
  "pus!", a tag being a big-endian 16-bit length with bit 7 marking a
  container, a 4-character name and its tags or value bytes. The tags
  are made up, as no real card's contents are known:
  <dcid><vers>01</vers><hwvr>MAJOR MINOR</hwvr></dcid>

